EXTRA = [
    {"after": "physiology", "id": "source", "nav": "Source water", "tag": "Physiology in depth", "h2": "Source water: why last year&rsquo;s validation does not cover today",
     "html": """
<p>A water system is designed and validated against the water that entered it during commissioning. The water that enters it today may be different. Every stage downstream has a fixed capacity. A change at the source can exhaust a stage that worked well for years.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Source or event</th><th>What changes</th><th>Consequence for the dialysis unit</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Municipal supply</td><td>Already disinfected with chlorine or chloramine. The utility may raise the dose, or switch from one disinfectant to the other, without telling its customers</td><td>A carbon bed sized for the old load is exhausted sooner; chloramine is adsorbed more slowly than chlorine and is the harder of the two to remove</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deep well</td><td>Usually not chlorinated at the source; often hard, and may carry iron, manganese, nitrate from fertilizer or sewage, and a high dissolved-mineral load</td><td>Softener and RO work harder; chemical analysis of the raw water matters more; any chlorination added on site becomes the unit&rsquo;s own responsibility to control</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dry season or supply interruption</td><td>The utility blends sources, pressure falls, and units buy water delivered by tanker</td><td>Tanker water has an unknown origin and unknown treatment; the worst recorded dialysis water disaster involved untreated reservoir water</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typhoon or flood</td><td>Turbidity, organic matter, and bacteria rise; utilities often respond with heavy extra chlorination</td><td>Sediment filters clog, carbon is exhausted early, and the bacterial load reaching the system increases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Raw-water storage tank or cistern</td><td>Water stands for hours or days, loses its disinfectant, and warms</td><td>Bacteria multiply before the water reaches the first filter; an unsealed tank adds debris and insects</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pipe repair or new construction nearby</td><td>Sediment and metals are released into the main</td><td>Sudden particle and chemical load</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The lesson is that safety comes from monitoring that never stops. A unit that passed its chemical analysis six months ago knows what its water was like six months ago. The unit keeps a relationship with its water provider and asks to be told about changes in treatment. Treat these events as reasons to test more often and to tell the technician and the medical director: a typhoon, a flood, a long brownout that stopped the loop, a tanker delivery, or a change in the taste, color, or smell of tap water. If you live in the same district as the unit, you may be the first to notice that the tap water smells strongly of chlorine that week. Bring that observation to the water room, not only to conversation.</p>
<p><strong>The water changes even when the system does not. A typhoon, a tanker, or a strange smell at the tap is a reason to test, and to tell someone.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "source", "id": "components", "nav": "Components in depth", "tag": "Physiology in depth", "h2": "What each component does, how it fails, and what shows the failure",
     "html": """
<p>The table earlier named the stages. This section explains each one well enough for you to read its log sheet with understanding. Many of these tasks belong to the technician. You need to know what a normal entry means and which abnormal entry stops treatment.</p>
<p><strong>Sediment and multimedia filters.</strong> Layers of sand and gravel, or cartridge filters, trap particles. As the filter loads, the pressure after it falls relative to the pressure before it. A widening pressure difference is the sign to backwash the bed or change the cartridge. Backwashing runs water backward through the bed to lift out the trapped material. It is timed for hours when no patient is on treatment.</p>
<p><strong>Softener.</strong> The tank holds resin beads coated with sodium. Calcium and magnesium bind to the resin more strongly than sodium does. They stay on the beads, and sodium enters the water. When the beads are full, hardness passes through. Regeneration floods the resin with concentrated salt solution (brine), which pushes the calcium and magnesium off to drain and reloads the beads with sodium. Three checks follow. The brine tank must contain salt. The regeneration timer must run outside treatment hours, so that brine never reaches patients. The water after the softener is tested for hardness at the frequency the unit sets. A sample taken at the end of the treatment day tells you most, because the resin is then most depleted. Hard water that escapes the softener scales the RO membrane, which lowers its output and its ability to reject contaminants.</p>
<p><strong>Carbon tanks.</strong> Granular activated carbon removes chlorine and chloramine by adsorption and chemical reaction on its surface. Two things decide whether it succeeds: how much unused carbon remains, and how long the water stays in contact with it. The second is the empty-bed contact time, the volume of the carbon bed divided by the water flow rate. Push more water through the same tanks, for example after stations are added, and contact time falls. Chloramine can then pass even through fresh carbon. That is why the tanks are arranged in series. The first tank (the worker) takes most of the load. The second (the polisher) is the reserve. A sampling port after the first tank shows when the worker is exhausted while the polisher still protects patients. A port after the second tank shows what is actually going forward. The unit&rsquo;s water policy says which port is tested when, and what happens when the first tank fails. Carbon also gives bacteria a rich surface, and it removes the disinfectant that was holding them down. So everything downstream of the carbon depends on RO, on continuous flow, and on disinfection for microbial control.</p>
<p><strong>Reverse osmosis.</strong> A pump forces water against a membrane that passes water molecules and holds back most dissolved salts, metals, bacteria, and endotoxin. The cleaned portion is the product water. The remainder carries the rejected material to drain. Two linked readings show performance continuously. The product-water monitor displays conductivity or total dissolved solids. The system also calculates percent rejection, which compares dissolved material in the feed water with what remains in the product. An intact membrane rejects well over 90% of dissolved ions. Falling rejection, or rising product conductivity, means the membrane is scaled, fouled, or damaged, or that the feed water has become more mineralized. Chlorine that passed the carbon is a classic cause of damage. This is an early warning that chemical contaminants are getting through, long before the next laboratory analysis. The alarm limit is set for each system. An alarm is a reason to stop and call, not to silence.</p>
<p><strong>Deionization tanks, where used.</strong> Some systems use resin tanks that exchange dissolved ions for hydrogen and hydroxide, as a polishing step or as a temporary substitute when the RO unit is out of service. These tanks carry a hazard that RO does not. Exhausted resin does not simply stop working. It can release ions it captured earlier, at concentrations higher than in the feed water. A fatal outbreak of fluoride poisoning occurred in exactly this way. A deionization tank therefore needs a continuous resistivity monitor with an alarm that keeps exhausted water from reaching patients. It also needs a filter downstream that retains bacteria and endotoxin, because resin beds do not remove microbes and readily grow them.</p>
<p><strong>Ultraviolet lamps and endotoxin-retentive filters.</strong> Ultraviolet light kills bacteria passing the lamp but does not remove them, and dead bacteria release endotoxin. A lamp is therefore followed by a filter fine enough to retain bacteria and endotoxin. Lamps lose output with age, and the quartz sleeve around them becomes coated, so lamp hours or intensity are logged. Filters are checked by the pressure difference across them and replaced or disinfected on schedule.</p>
<p><strong>Storage and the distribution loop.</strong> Treated water has no disinfectant in it. Its protection against bacteria is movement and regular disinfection. The loop circulates continuously and returns unused water. It is built without dead legs, the capped or rarely used branches where water stands and biofilm forms. An isolation room or a seldom-used station is a typical dead leg. A product-water storage tank, if present, has a tight lid, a filtered air vent, and a base that drains completely. Piping that touches treated water is made of inert material. Copper, brass, galvanized steel, and aluminum are avoided, because pure water dissolves metal from them. A long brownout or a holiday closure that stops the loop is a period of stagnation. The technician decides whether disinfection and testing are needed before patients return.</p>
<p><strong>Each stage has a reading that shows it failing. Know which reading belongs to which stage, and which one stops treatment.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "components", "id": "syndromes", "nav": "Contaminant syndromes", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "From contaminant to bedside: syndromes and the cluster rule",
     "html": """
<p>You will never see a contaminant. You will see a patient with a symptom. The table links the two. Most of this knowledge comes from outbreak reports, and the limits in the standard were set to prevent a repeat.[[iso23500_3]]</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Contaminant</th><th>Usual origin</th><th>What the patient shows</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Chloramine, chlorine</td><td>Utility disinfection; exhausted carbon</td><td>Hemolysis: chest or back pain, breathlessness, dark or translucent blood in the lines. With lower, continuing exposure: falling hemoglobin and anemia that does not respond to treatment across many patients</td></tr>
<tr><td>Aluminum</td><td>Added by some utilities to clear water; aluminum parts</td><td>Over months: speech disturbance, muscle jerks, seizures, dementia; bone pain and fractures; small-cell anemia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fluoride</td><td>Utility fluoridation; release from exhausted deionization resin</td><td>Itching, nausea, headache, chest pain, then fatal heart rhythm disturbance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Calcium and magnesium</td><td>Hard water passing a failed softener and RO</td><td>&ldquo;Hard water syndrome&rdquo;: nausea, vomiting, headache, flushing, high blood pressure, weakness and lethargy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Copper, zinc</td><td>Copper or galvanized pipes, brass fittings, especially with acidic water</td><td>Hemolysis, nausea, chills, fever</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nitrate</td><td>Well water contaminated by fertilizer or sewage</td><td>Methemoglobinemia: bluish or gray color with brownish blood, low blood pressure, nausea</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sulfate</td><td>Source water</td><td>Nausea, vomiting, metabolic acidosis</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bacteria and endotoxin</td><td>Biofilm in tanks, loop, bicarbonate containers, machine fluid path, reprocessed dialyzers</td><td>Chills, fever, muscle aches, low blood pressure during treatment; bloodstream infection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cyanobacterial toxin</td><td>Algal bloom in a surface reservoir</td><td>Visual disturbance, nausea, vomiting, then liver failure</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The last row is the 1996 outbreak in Caruaru, Brazil. Over four days, 116 of 130 patients at one dialysis center had visual disturbance, nausea, and vomiting during treatment. One hundred and one developed acute liver failure, and 50 died. Both of the town&rsquo;s dialysis centers drew water from the same reservoir. The water for the unaffected center was treated, filtered, and chlorinated. The water for the affected center was not. Toxins made by cyanobacteria were found in that water and in patients&rsquo; blood and liver tissue.[[caruaru1998]] The investigation was an outbreak cohort study, and the link between the water and the deaths is about as direct as evidence gets. The lesson for a Philippine unit that buys tanker water in the dry season or after a typhoon is plain. Water of unknown origin is a clinical risk, and its use is a decision for the medical director, not a logistics matter.</p>
<p><strong>The cluster rule.</strong> One patient with chills usually has a patient-level cause, most often a catheter infection. Two or more patients on the same shift with the same new symptom have something in common. What they share is the water, the dialysate, a batch of concentrate or dialyzers, or a reprocessing step. Your reasoning changes at that moment:</p>
<ul>
<li>Say it aloud to the charge nurse: &ldquo;This is the second patient with the same symptom.&rdquo; Clusters are missed because each nurse sees only one patient.</li>
<li>Do not connect further patients until the common cause has been considered.</li>
<li>Assess the affected patients, and stop or continue their treatments as the unit protocol and the physician direct. If hemolysis is suspected, the blood is not returned (Module 9).</li>
<li>Notify the nephrologist, the technician, and the medical director at once.</li>
<li>Keep the evidence: dialyzers, lines, concentrate containers with their lot numbers, and samples of water and dialysate collected as the protocol states.</li>
<li>Record for every patient the station, machine, dialyzer type and reuse number, concentrate lots, and time of onset. The pattern across patients locates the source.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each patient&rsquo;s own possible causes are still investigated, with blood cultures where ordered. The rule sets the order of thinking. With a cluster, consider the shared exposure first, because every additional patient connected to it is a new exposure.</p>
<p><strong>One patient with a symptom is a patient problem. Two patients with the same symptom on one shift share an exposure until proven otherwise.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "evidence", "id": "micro", "nav": "Monitoring and roles", "tag": "Evidence", "h2": "Microbiological monitoring, trends, and who is responsible for what",
     "html": """
<p><strong>Why the method matters.</strong> The bacteria that live in purified water are adapted to cold, nutrient-poor conditions. Cultured on rich hospital media at body temperature for two days, many fail to grow, and the result looks falsely reassuring. The dialysis water standard therefore specifies low-nutrient culture media incubated at a low temperature for several days. It also requires a separate assay for endotoxin, because endotoxin can be high when the bacterial count is low.[[iso23500_3]] A laboratory that does not use a dialysis-appropriate method can report &ldquo;no growth&rdquo; on water that is out of specification. You do not perform the culture. You can ask whether the laboratory uses the method named in the unit&rsquo;s adopted standard.</p>
<p><strong>How samples are collected.</strong> The principles are the same whoever collects them:</p>
<ul>
<li>Samples come from designated sample ports chosen to represent what patients receive: typically where the loop begins and ends, at outlets that feed machines, and where water is used for reprocessing or for mixing concentrate. The unit&rsquo;s water plan lists them.</li>
<li>The port is opened and allowed to run before collecting, so that the sample represents water flowing in the loop and not water standing in the port.</li>
<li>Collection is aseptic, into sterile, endotoxin-free containers, without touching the inside of the cap or the stream.</li>
<li>Samples are taken at the point in the disinfection cycle that the plan specifies. A sample taken immediately after disinfection shows the system at its best and hides a problem.</li>
<li>Samples reach the laboratory promptly, or are refrigerated as the laboratory instructs, because bacteria multiply in a warm sample during transport.</li>
<li>Each sample is labeled with site, date, time, and collector.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Trending and the action level.</strong> Philippine guidance requires bacteriological testing at least monthly and chemical analysis at least every six months.[[doh2013]][[psn2024]] A single result is a snapshot. The series is the information. A count that rises month over month while still within the limit shows biofilm becoming established. Responding then is cheaper and safer. That is the purpose of the action level: a result at or above it starts corrective steps while the water is still acceptable for use. The steps include disinfecting the loop, looking for a dead leg, and retesting. A result above the maximum limit is a different event. It raises the question of whether treatment continues on that water, and that decision belongs to the medical director under the unit&rsquo;s policy.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Typical responsibilities</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Water technician</td><td>Operates and maintains the system; performs daily checks and the total chlorine and hardness tests; backwashes, regenerates, and disinfects; collects samples; keeps the logs; reports any abnormal reading immediately</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nurse in charge of the shift</td><td>Confirms before the first connection that the required checks are recorded and within limits; holds treatment when they are not; recognizes symptoms that suggest a water or dialysate problem; applies the cluster rule; notifies and documents</td></tr>
<tr><td>Staff nurse</td><td>Performs machine and dialyzer checks at the station; never overrides a dialysate alarm; reports symptoms and anything unusual about the water or the machine</td></tr>
<tr><td>Medical director</td><td>Accountable for the quality of water and dialysate; approves the water policy and the adopted standard; reviews results; decides on continuing, suspending, or relocating treatment when limits are exceeded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Supplier or biomedical engineer</td><td>Preventive maintenance, membrane and media replacement, repairs, and revalidation after changes to the system</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>What you must know even when someone else tests.</strong> Where the logs are kept, and what a complete day looks like. The unit&rsquo;s limit and action level for total chlorine, bacteria, and endotoxin. The date and result of the last cultures and chemical analysis. When the loop and the machines were last disinfected. Whom to call, at any hour, when a reading is abnormal.</p>
<p><strong>Documentation and the quality meeting.</strong> Water and dialysate results are reviewed at the unit&rsquo;s regular quality meeting with infection, reaction, and incident data. A rising culture count can then be read beside a rise in fevers during treatment. For each result, record the value, whether it crossed the action level, what was done, who was told, and the retest result. A corrective action without a recorded retest is unfinished.</p>
<p><strong>Delegating a test does not delegate the duty to know it was done. Read the trend, not only the last result.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "bedside", "id": "dialysate", "nav": "Dialysate and alarms", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "Dialysate preparation and dialysate-side alarms",
     "html": """
<p><strong>Two concentrates, mixed at the machine.</strong> Dialysate needs bicarbonate as its buffer, and it also needs calcium and magnesium. At high concentration these cannot share a container, because calcium and magnesium carbonate would precipitate. So they are supplied separately. The acid concentrate holds sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium, chloride, usually glucose, and a small amount of acid. The bicarbonate concentrate holds sodium bicarbonate. The machine draws each one and dilutes both with treated water in fixed proportions. The small amount of acid keeps the final mixture at a pH where the minerals stay dissolved.</p>
<p><strong>Concentrates must match the machine.</strong> Manufacturers use different proportioning ratios, and each concentrate is made for one ratio. A concentrate made for a different ratio produces dialysate of the wrong composition. The conductivity display may not show the error, because conductivity measures total ions and cannot tell which ions they are. Before you connect, read the label for three things. It is the formulation prescribed for this patient, potassium and calcium content in particular. It is made for this machine&rsquo;s proportioning system. It is in date and intact. Where unit policy requires an independent check of conductivity or pH with a handheld meter or test strip before treatment, that check guards against a machine sensor that has drifted out of calibration.</p>
<p><strong>Bicarbonate concentrate grows bacteria.</strong> Acid concentrate is too acidic and too salty to support growth. Bicarbonate solution is neither, and it is an excellent culture medium. It also loses carbon dioxide on standing, which raises its pH. For both reasons, mix bicarbonate fresh or take it from sealed cartridges or bags. Use it within the period the manufacturer and the unit policy allow, and never top it up from one day to the next. Empty, rinse, and disinfect reusable jugs and pickup tubes on the unit&rsquo;s schedule, and store them dry and inverted. Discard leftover bicarbonate.</p>
<p><strong>Alarms on the dialysate side.</strong> When dialysate is out of range, the machine goes into bypass. Dialysate is diverted to drain before it reaches the dialyzer, while the blood pump continues. Bypass protects the patient. Dialysis and fluid removal pause while it lasts, so a long bypass is lost treatment time, and you record it. Clear the alarm by correcting the cause, never by widening limits or overriding.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Alarm</th><th>What it means</th><th>Common causes</th><th>First response</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Conductivity low</td><td>Dialysate too dilute. If it reached the blood, water would enter red cells and cause hemolysis</td><td>Empty or disconnected concentrate container; pickup tube not seated or kinked; wrong concentrate; proportioning fault</td><td>Confirm the machine is in bypass. Check both containers and connections. If the cause is not found and corrected promptly, stop using the machine and call the technician</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conductivity high</td><td>Dialysate too concentrated. Sodium loading causes thirst, headache, and high blood pressure, and at extremes shrinks cells</td><td>Wrong concentrate or connectors exchanged; low water inflow or pressure; proportioning fault</td><td>As above. Check the water supply to the machine. Do not resume until readings are stable and, where policy requires, independently confirmed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temperature high or low</td><td>Overheated dialysate causes hemolysis; cold dialysate causes chills and shivering</td><td>Heater or sensor fault; inlet water temperature or flow problem</td><td>Bypass. Assess the patient. Do not resume until the temperature is within range; persistent alarm takes the machine out of service</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blood-leak detector</td><td>A sensor in the used dialysate has detected what may be blood, from a broken fiber in the dialyzer</td><td>Membrane rupture; false alarms from air bubbles or a dirty sensor</td><td>Look at the dialysate outflow line. Test the used dialysate with a blood test strip. If a leak is confirmed, treatment through that dialyzer stops; whether the blood is returned depends on the size of the leak and the unit protocol. Never disable the detector</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transmembrane pressure high</td><td>The machine needs an unusually large pressure difference to achieve the set fluid removal</td><td>Clotting fibers; kinked dialysate line; fluid removal rate too high for the dialyzer</td><td>Inspect the dialyzer and chambers for clotting and the lines for kinks. Review the fluid removal setting against the order. Report</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transmembrane pressure low, or flow error</td><td>Pressure or flow on the dialysate side is not as expected</td><td>Loose dialyzer connector, leak, air in the dialysate path</td><td>Check connectors and look for fluid leaks; call the technician if it recurs</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>With every dialysate alarm, look at the patient before the screen. Then ask whether the same alarm is sounding on neighboring machines. The same alarm on several machines points to the water or to a batch of concentrate, and the cluster rule applies.</p>
<p><strong>An alarm is the machine protecting the patient. Correct the cause. Never widen the limit to make the sound stop.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "dialysate", "id": "disinfect-reuse", "nav": "Disinfection and reuse", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "Machine disinfection and dialyzer reprocessing",
     "html": """
<p><strong>Inside the machine.</strong> The fluid path of a dialysis machine is warm and wet and carries bicarbonate. It will grow biofilm unless it is disinfected regularly. Machines run heat cycles, chemical cycles, or both, at the frequency the manufacturer and the unit policy state. Descaling or acid cleaning removes the carbonate deposits that shelter bacteria and blunt disinfection.</p>
<p><strong>Residual disinfectant testing.</strong> A chemical cycle fills the fluid path with a substance that would injure blood if it crossed the membrane. Hemolysis, chest pain, breathlessness, and falling blood pressure have followed treatments begun on a poorly rinsed machine. The rinse cycle is designed to remove the chemical. A brownout, a low water supply, or a person in a hurry can interrupt it. The only proof is a test of the rinse water with the strip made for that chemical. Do it after the rinse and before the dialyzer is primed, and read it at the time the strip&rsquo;s instructions state. Treat a machine as still containing disinfectant if it shows a chemical-cycle message, was found powered off mid-cycle, or has no recorded test. Rinse it and test again before use.</p>
<p><strong>Outside the machine.</strong> Between patients, clean and disinfect the external surfaces of the machine, the chair or bed, the table, and other frequently touched surfaces. Discard items taken to the station, dedicate them to that patient, or disinfect them before they return to a common area. Change pressure transducer protectors if they become wet. A machine whose internal protector may have been contaminated with blood is taken out of service for inspection.[[cdc2001]] This is hepatitis prevention as much as tidiness, because hepatitis B virus (HBV) survives on dry surfaces.</p>
<p><strong>Dialyzer reprocessing.</strong> Many Philippine units reuse dialyzers, for the same patient only. Reuse is acceptable only as a controlled process. Each check in it answers a specific hazard.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Hazard it controls</th><th>What you verify</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Permanent label with the patient&rsquo;s full name, a second identifier, and the reuse count</td><td>Use on the wrong patient, which exposes the patient to another person&rsquo;s blood</td><td>The label is legible and matches this patient. Two people check it, and the patient is one of them whenever able</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fiber-bundle volume test</td><td>Loss of clearance as fibers clot. A dialyzer is retired when the volume falls below 80% of its original value</td><td>The reprocessing record shows a pass for the last use</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pressure or leak test</td><td>Broken fibers and blood leak</td><td>Recorded pass</td></tr>
<tr><td>Germicide fill and storage for the required contact time</td><td>Surviving bacteria; pyrogenic reaction or bloodstream infection</td><td>Germicide <em>presence</em> test is positive before rinsing, showing the dialyzer was actually filled; storage time is within the limits of the unit procedure</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rinse and priming before use</td><td>Germicide reaching the patient</td><td>Germicide <em>absence</em> test is negative after rinsing, performed as the procedure states and recorded with the tester&rsquo;s name</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inspection</td><td>Cracks, clots, discoloration, missing or loose caps</td><td>The dialyzer looks clean and intact; if in doubt, it is discarded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maximum number of uses</td><td>Membrane fatigue</td><td>The count is within unit policy</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The two germicide tests are easily confused, and they answer opposite questions. Presence before rinsing proves disinfection happened. Absence after rinsing proves the patient will not receive the germicide. Both are needed. Residual germicide usually shows itself in the first minutes of treatment: burning along the access vein, chest tightness, breathlessness, or a fall in blood pressure. The response is that of a dialyzer reaction in Module 9.</p>
<p>Patients positive for hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) are excluded from dialyzer reuse programs, because reprocessing their dialyzers exposes staff and shared equipment to a highly infectious virus.[[cdc2001]] Unit policy states how patients with other bloodborne infections are handled. The person who reprocesses dialyzers wears protective equipment against both blood and chemical splash, and works in a ventilated room. The site&rsquo;s reuse guide in the reading list describes the process in full. The unit&rsquo;s own procedure governs.</p>
<p><strong>Two people, two identifiers, two germicide tests. If any one is missing, the dialyzer does not reach the patient.</strong></p>
"""},
]
